<div id=3D"expander-content-1840519918" class=3D"expand-content">
<p><span>AI =EA=B2=80=EC=83=89 =EA=B2=B0=EA=B3=BC - DTO class=EB=8A=94 quer=
y=ED=95=9C column=EB=93=A4=EC=9D=84 constructor=EB=A1=9C =ED=98=B8=EC=B6=9C=
=EC=8B=9C =EB=AA=A8=EB=91=90 =EC=A0=84=EB=8B=AC=ED=95=98=EC=97=AC instance =
=EC=83=9D=EC=84=B1</span></p>
<p><span>The error "Result class must have a single constructor with exactl=
y parameters with dto class" when using Spring Boot, Kotlin, and JPA with D=
TOs typically arises when you are attempting to project query results direc=
tly into a DTO using a constructor expression (e.g., in a JPQL or native qu=
ery) and the DTO's constructor signature does not precisely match the selec=
ted columns and their order.</span><br><span>JPA, as a specification, requi=
res persistent entities (classes annotated with&nbsp;<code class=3D"mv6bHd"=
>@Entity</code>) to have a no-argument constructor.&nbsp;</span><span>This =
is because JPA providers (like Hibernate) use reflection to instantiate ent=
ity objects.&nbsp;</span><span>When loading data from the database, the pro=
vider first creates an instance of the entity using its no-arg constructor =
and then populates its fields using setters or by direct field access.&nbsp=
;</span><span>This process ensures that the entity can be managed by the pe=
rsistence context.</span><br><span>However, when you are working with DTOs =
for projections, the situation is different.&nbsp;</span><span>DTOs are not=
 entities managed by JPA;&nbsp;</span><span>they are simple data holders.&n=
bsp;</span><span>If you are using a constructor expression in a query to di=
rectly populate a DTO, the JPA provider expects a constructor in your DTO t=
hat precisely matches the types and order of the columns selected in your q=
uery.</span><br><span>Why JPA uses a default no-argument constructor for en=
tities:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><p><span><strong>Reflection-based instantiation:</strong>&nbsp;</span><=
br><span>JPA providers use&nbsp;<code class=3D"mv6bHd">Class.newInstance()<=
/code>&nbsp;(or similar reflection mechanisms) to create new instances of e=
ntities.&nbsp;</span><span>This method requires a no-argument constructor t=
o be present.</span></p></li>
<li><p><span><strong>Flexibility in object creation:</strong>&nbsp;</span><=
br><span>By using a no-argument constructor, the JPA provider can create an=
 empty entity instance and then populate its fields individually, which is =
crucial for various JPA operations like lazy loading, proxy creation, and d=
etached entity management.</span></p></li>
</ul>
<p><br><span>Why DTOs need a precise constructor for projections:</span></p=
>
<ul>
<li><p><span><strong>Direct mapping:</strong>&nbsp;</span><br><span>When us=
ing constructor expressions in queries to populate DTOs, the DTO's construc=
tor acts as a direct mapping target for the query results.&nbsp;</span><spa=
n>The types and order of the constructor parameters must exactly match the =
selected columns.</span></p></li>
<li><p><span><strong>No reflection-based field population:</strong>&nbsp;</=
span><br><span>Unlike entities, DTOs are not typically populated field by f=
ield after instantiation in this scenario.&nbsp;</span><span>Instead, the e=
ntire DTO instance is constructed in one go using the provided constructor =
arguments.</span></p></li>
</ul>
<p><br><span>To resolve the DTO constructor error:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><p><span><strong>Ensure constructor signature matches query:</strong>&n=
bsp;</span><br><span>Verify that the constructor in your DTO class has para=
meters that exactly correspond to the types and order of the columns you ar=
e selecting in your query.</span></p></li>
<li><p><span>Consider using the&nbsp;<code class=3D"mv6bHd">no-arg</code>&n=
